+        // CFM does NOT index the export hash table with a plain mask. It folds the hash word:
+        //
+        //     index = (hashWord ^ (hashWord >> tablePower)) & (tableSize - 1)
+        //
+        // This code used `key % sz`. The two agree only while bit `tablePower` of the word is
+        // clear, so every symbol with that bit set was written into a bucket CFM never looks in:
+        // present in the fragment, named in the .exp file, and invisible to FindSymbol.
+        //
+        // The loop above keeps ONE bucket until a fragment has ten exports, which is why this
+        // went unnoticed for so long -- with sz == 1 both rules trivially give 0. The bug appears
+        // at the tenth export, and it strands whichever symbols happen to have the bit set, NOT
+        // the one just added. Found 2026-09-21: adding a tenth export to an OS 9 AirPort driver
+        // silently broke two accessors that had not been touched in weeks, and CFM reported them
+        // as missing -- indistinguishable from a forgotten line in the .exp file.
+        //
+        // Verified against that hardware run: the fold predicts exactly which symbols CFM can and
+        // cannot find, 9 of 9 correct, while the plain mask predicts all ten resolve and is
+        // contradicted twice. Two other candidate folds were tested and contradicted 8 and 6
+        // times respectively.
+        //
+        // This is a no-op for any fragment with nine or fewer exports (sz == 1, so both rules
+        // yield 0) -- i.e. for every fragment previously built with this tool.
+        const size_t power = power_;
+        auto bucket = [power, sz](uint32_t key) -> size_t {
+            return (size_t)((key ^ (key >> power)) & (uint32_t)(sz - 1));
+        };
